Lisa (lnemzer1@nycap.rr.com) wrote:
: I have been trying to install a DEC Print Server 20.  A transceiver has been
: connected to the AUI port on the rear of the unit, and connected by CAT5
: 10BaseT cable to an open port on an HP hub.
: 
: As the printer warms up it goes through a series of internal checks,
: counting down from 9 to 0.  After 0, two addresses are displayed:
: 
: 08-00-2b-15-43-51 and
: 2.31.21.1
: 
: I assume these to be the tcp/ip address for the unit.

The first line is the Ethernet (MAC) address for the printer.  The second line
is a series of version numbers for various firmware components inside the
printer.  You formatted the second line wrong.  It's: "2.3 1.2 1.1".

: I installed a turbo printserver 20 printer under NT.  I created a new port
: and entered the tcp/ip address above.  There is definitely some
: communication through the hub from both the printer and the server (an IBM
: 325 if that matters).
: 
: After a few minutes the printer displays the message: ?45.  I presume this
: to be an error message.  Would anyone know what this means?

The printer displayed "?54", which is "I sent out a boot request on the
network, but no one gave me a boot within a reasonable amount of time".  That
is, you don't have a boot server for the printer.

: Also, I installed DEC Prism 5.1.  The monitor reports an inability to
: communicate with the printer.

That's because the printer, upon successful completion of the powerup testing,
broadcasts a boot request to load its software.  You never mentioned anything
about setting up a boot server, so I'll assume that you've not done so.  

Typically, the PrintServer 20 boots from a VMS boot server, but with later
versions of the controller firmware, it will boot from a TCP/IP bootp server.
You'll need for the boot server the software to communicate to the printer, as
well as the software for the printer itself.  I can't help you there, though.

        Stu



