Lisa wrote:
> 
> I have been trying to install a DEC Print Server 20.  A transceiver has been
> connected to the AUI port on the rear of the unit, and connected by CAT5
> 10BaseT cable to an open port on an HP hub.
> 
> As the printer warms up it goes through a series of internal checks,
> counting down from 9 to 0.  After 0, two addresses are displayed:
> 
> 08-00-2b-15-43-51 and
> 2.31.21.1
> 
> I assume these to be the tcp/ip address for the unit.

The first line is the printer's Ethernet hardware address.

The second line does not look correct.  It should have the format:

	X.X X.X X.X

These would be the version numbers of the controller, print engine, and
large capacity paper feeder.

 
> I installed a turbo printserver 20 printer under NT.  I created a new port
> and entered the tcp/ip address above.  There is definitely some
> communication through the hub from both the printer and the server (an IBM
> 325 if that matters).
> 
> After a few minutes the printer displays the message: ?45.  I presume this
> to be an error message.  Would anyone know what this means?

Are you sure that you didn't get a 54 instead of a 45?  ?54 means that
no host on the network downloaded the printer.

 
> Also, I installed DEC Prism 5.1.  The monitor reports an inability to
> communicate with the printer.
> 
> Any help would be greatly appreciated.


There are several pieces of software required to use a LPS20.

When the printer is turned on, it "knows" how to do its self tests, but
not how to print.

When it completes its self tests, it will send a request on the network
asking for someone to tell it what to do.  Some host on the network must
download the software to the LPS20.  This is the "PrintServer
Software".  This only needs to be done once each time it is powered up. 
The load host may be most anything.  Check the URL:

http://www.digital.com/printers/sofwar/prt_swdrv_printserver.html

When you configure the PrintServer Software in the load host, you will
need to enter the printer's Ethernet address (found on the front panel
when it boots) so that the load host and the printer can communicate.

Once the printer has been loaded, any machine on the network may print
to it.  For example, you want to print to it from NT.  You could use
your NT box as a load host or the load host might be a Unix or VMS
system somewhere else on the network.  Even if the VMS system loaded the
software to the printer, the NT system can still use the printer.

The printer drivers you load under NT allow NT to print to the printer. 
They do not configure NT as a load host.

PRISM allows you to monitor and control the printer once it has booted
and is running the PrintServer Software.


There are a couple of other gotchas to watch out for.

I think that the LPS20 is sensitive to heart-beat.  I don't remember for
sure which way it should be set, but I suspect that it should be on. 
There should be a switch on your transceiver that controls this.  Some
transceivers label this switch SQE.

Normally, the controller board switches should be set to switch 1 down
and switches 2, 3, and 4 up.  If you flip down the control panel, you
will find a cutout with some LEDs and the controller board switches. 
Switch 1 is the leftmost and switch 4 is the rightmost.

If you are using a "normal" LPS20 (and not a turbo LPS20), and the
PrintServer Software is being loaded from a TCP/IP host, i.e., not from
VMS, switch 4 must be set down.

Good luck!

