Actually 24.3/18.4=1.32, or 32% faster.  The Alpha/Intel
ratio of 26/24.3 is nearly 7% faster than the Xeon, and
HPs published number of 32.6 on the J5000 is over 34%
faster than the Xeon.  <Insert standard disclaimer about
scalability with regards to memory bandwidth and latency
along with problems which are better served with larger
register sets, etc... here.>  The fact remains that for a
certain set of problems, many of the RISC architectures
(most of which were flat out faster than the Intel line
on everything) have not been able to keep up with the
IA32 architecture.  This stands against what was
considered the common wisdom of the day a few years back
that the simplicity of RISC was going to allow it to
scale further and faster than the CISC architectures.

Its also interesting to note the floating point numbers
on the PowerPC vs the Xeon from:

    http://www.chips.ibm.com/products/powerpc/perform.html
    http://www.spec.org/osg/cpu95/results/res99q2/cpu95-19990426-03549.html

The 550MHz Xeon in a shipping system turns in 16.8 SPECfp95
vs only 13.8 in the 450MHz PowerPC 750 (which is higher
than their quoted number for the 466MHz 750 at only 12.6.
Good luck figuring out what kind of configuration IBM
used for these numbers..)  For this set of benchmarks the
Xeon is nearly 22% faster than what one would assume to
be the fastest PowerPC configuration.  None of these
numbers are impressive against the HPs, Alphas, MIPS
or UltraSPARCs top numbers.

>Of course, none of these systems run the same software, so 
