One quick note - 550Mhz/450MHz = 1.22 or 22 % percent faster
just by clock speed.  Is this the 22% speed differential.

Now regarding computer architecture and Process Technology.
I am curious how much of this speed is due to Intels prowess
with its silicon processing technology or IBMs lack of silicon
processing technology (even with copper and SOI at its disposal,
which I don't believe IBM has any problems with silicon processing),
or Intel's massive manpower to use a large crew of circuit
designers to implement more dynamic logic in more critical paths
which IA32 must have over PowerPC.

Is the PowerPC architecture
just harder to crank up the clock speed on than IA32 (I doubt it, but If
so why?), IBM also is having problems with cranking up the clock speed
on the Power3 which if it was at 400MHZ or higher I bet (with linear scaling)
would be in the top with Alph and HP  PA8500. Now maybe the
complex 8 way issue Power 3 with its 2 FP units is just so complex that
scaling up clock speed is too difficult.

It seems to me that Intel has a golden
egg in the P6 core and that with about 6 years (or more) of investement in
that core (Pentium Pro to Pentium III, and all of its variations in between)
that  Intel has spent a lot of money for a lot of methodology and circuit design
to crank up its clock speed, maybe the other vendors cannot not make such
investments as quickly as Intel, for instance all of this RISCs from HP, Sun
and Mips are slower clock speeds than Intel. All of the ones with slower
clock speeds are slower on SPECint95 except HP which has a very very
large, low latency L1 cache on chip), Alpha is the only RISC with clock speeds
comparable to or faster than Intel.

Is RISC running out of steam, I don't think so, Is RISC running out of money
maybe, It is difficult( but not impossible) to compete with Intel and there P6
core that they have tweaked to its end( The Coppermine core will be the last
with the P6 microarchitecture).

ISA, Microarchitecture, Process Technology, Cache Design, System Interface
all contribute to the final speed. Is there any vendor with the best of all of
these
traits that sets them so far apart from the rest, If so I can't see it. So many
benchmarks, so many systems, so many configurations.

Back to the orignal argument - I think that PowerPCs and Power3's problem
is there clock speed, not there architecture, especially versus IA32
(which is horrific) I think that PowerPCs and Power3s architecture limit
the clock speed somewhat, PowerPC tries to do wider issue (decode and
issue to Reservation Stations instead of a central but deeper buffer)
that Intel but the Dispatch rates are about the same.

One more thing to consider - What are the relative die sizes, yields, and prices
for these processors. Just curious.


All opinions are my own.

