In article <37507FF7.415D1017@eng.sun.com>, cagomez@sun.eng.com wrote:

> Is the PowerPC architecture just harder to crank up the clock speed
> on than IA32 (I doubt it, but If so why?)

I don't think so.  The problem is that for several years now there hasn't
been a PowerPC designed for workstation-type applications, and especially
not for FP applications.  Basically, there has been no follow-on chip to
the PPC604e, which at 350 MHz several years ago was a pretty hot performer
at FP.

The PowerPC 750 is a nice low power, cheap, small chip for embedded and
portable applications, but it was never intended for serious workstation
applications.  The FPU, for example, is seriously limited compared to the
one on the 604e -- or even the old 601.  The fact that it comes within
spitting distance of everything else on the desktop is amazing.


> PowerPC tries to do wider issue (decode and
> issue to Reservation Stations instead of a central but deeper buffer)
> that Intel but the Dispatch rates are about the same.

The PowerPC 750 doesn't do wide issue at all -- just two instructions
wide, with some branches picked off earlier in the fetch buffer.

The 604/604e did wide dispatch, but had a huge bottleneck in the
completion stage, where only two integer results could be written back to
registers each cycle.  On every bit of code I've optomised on the 604,
this was the limiting factor.  I think the wide dispatch was intended just
to try to ensure an average of two-at-a-time on old unscheduled code.  A
PPC604e with the existing front end, increased writeback ability (even
three per cycle would really help), and the 750's backside cache interface
would fly pretty well.


> Is RISC running out of steam, I don't think so, Is RISC running out of
> money maybe

That's more likely to be the problem.

-- Bruce



