> The 550MHz Xeon in a shipping system turns in 16.8 SPECfp95
> vs only 13.8 in the 450MHz PowerPC 750 (which is higher
> than their quoted number for the 466MHz 750 at only 12.6.
> Good luck figuring out what kind of configuration IBM
> used for these numbers..)  For this set of benchmarks the
> Xeon is nearly 22% faster than what one would assume to
> be the fastest PowerPC configuration.  None of these
> numbers are impressive against the HPs, Alphas, MIPS
> or UltraSPARCs top numbers.

The PPC 750 has a single FP unit which takes
an extra cycle for double precision multplies compared
to single precision multiplies (which are three cycles).  
No doubt this effects things considerably.  Note that the
NorthStar PowerPC processor which is used in the RS/6000 H70
which also has a single FPU (but with a full double
precision multiplier) operating at 340MHz gets a SPECfp95
of 20+, and the POWER3 based systems (which are PowerPC's)
with two FPUs with double precision multiply running at 200MHz
gets SPECfp95 of 30.  The low numbers for the PPC 750 have
little to do with not being able to keep up with Intel,
but rather with design trades offs made to keep die sizes
and power consumption down.




