21264 numbers are from a .35 micron part and therefore don't make a good
comparison point for the statement above(thats not to say they shouldn't
be compared in the market).  Yet the Alpha on a process that is probably
a gen and a half behind the Xeon still out performs it.

Getting onto the Sparc,PowerX(which is a lot better than talking about
power1/2/3/AS/PC), and MIPS the comparison doesn't look so good.  So
lets delve into it.

The Power3 is probably the comparison point for the Xeon instead of the
PC.  The Power3 is currently in a psuedo .25 micron process, knowing a
little about how IBM does things, this is probably around a half gen
behind the xeon's process.  If IBM manages to hit their speed target for
the .22 part(which is aparently in field testing currently although I
haven't heard any rumors of what the speed is) they should be able to at
least meet the Xeon in int performance and on the FP side it gets kinda
silly.  

MIPS is on EOL(at least as far as SGI is concerned, MIPS inc has other
ideas).  It is widely acknowledged that the r10k had a lot of problems
and on top of that management issues that kept this shrink schedule
messed up.  Needless to say(and meaning as little offense as possible)
it really hasn't kept competitive.  The might have changed if
beast/alien/el capitan didn't get cancelled, but SGI decided to throw
down with intel. 

Sparc is a weird beast.  In a lot of ways SUN has been able to get where
they are because of the mis-steps of others.  Sparc has almost always
lagged and it hasn't been a real issue.  We'll have to wait for US3 to
come out and see if it can generate the performance they SUN claims.  If
it does then x86 will likely be at the back of the pack once again.  

And lets not forget the resources poured into x86 by intel.  It seems as
if the size of their design teams is 3 times that of most other
projects.  Whether this is poor management, inexperience, or nessesary,
I'm not really qualified to say.  But it does exist. 

A final issue to consider is that the gargantuan transistor budgets can
be a HUGE equalization fact.  In areas of the market where those budgets
aren't available, x86 really doesn't play today.  The embedded market is
one such area.  It is rappidly being dominated by simple, fast, small,
low power risc chips.  I personally don't think it will be long before
the volume of ARM, MIPS, SH, PowerPC etc out sell the x86s.  



Aaron Spink
not speaking for Compaq





