Please read the part following that sentence. I was not making an argument
for megahertz to megahertz comparison for the general case, I just noticed
in this case that the 22% speed differential (SPECfp) also just happened to match
the 22% difference in clock speed. I NEVER stated that a clock speed
comparison is valid in general or that it could be used in a general sense.

I also just commented on the fact that most RISCs are running a lower clock
speed than the Intel Processors (which are actually a CISC frontend with
a superscalar RISC like core) And could this lead to the SPEC advantage
that Intel has over the lower clock speed RISCS , again the exception is the
HP PA8500 @ 440MHz with a 2 cycle latency 1.5 MB L1 cache (512KB ICACHE,
1 MB DCACHE) gets very high SPEC marks.

The ONLY real speed comparison is the EXECUTION TIME of YOUR application.

So the combination of Process Technology, Computer Architecture, and Circuit
design leads to the final speed of the processor. I was just trying to Identify
which design space ( Process Technology, Computer Architecture, and Circuit
design) is responsible for Intels Speed - I believe that it is due to Intels process

technology and heavy investment of circuit designers on the critical paths.

All opinions are my own.

