> to initialize a volume, or mount a volume. I've tried to use the "MOUNT
> /FOREIGN"
> command while logged in as SYSTEM, still with no success. What do I need
> to do to
> get this drive up and running?  The drive is a SCSI-2 Narrow
> configuration, and it spins
> up nicely at all the right times. It seems like the Vaxstation is at
> least accessing it. I am
> going to use the drive as the boot drive when I get my OpenVMS 7.2
> Hobbyist CD-ROM
> in the mail.


Some versions of VMS have problems with the INQUIRY strings returned by
certain SCSI drives.  I do remember that some of the offending drives
were Seagate drives.  It appears that you may have one of these.

I think the problem was fixed in VMS 6.2.  There may be an ECO for 6.1
to fix this problem.  In any case, VMS 7.2 probably won't have any
problems.

On a slightly different topic, all VAXstation 3100s have a 1GB
limitation on the size of the system disk.  It appears that this drive
is very close to the limit.  I think that the exact limit is that the
drive must have 2,097,151 or fewer 512 byte sectors.  Depending on how
you count gigabytes, this may be 1.073, 1.048, or 1.023 GB.

I would recommend that you check this out before using this drive as a
system drive.  Using a drive that is too large doesn't generate any
error messages and appears to work, but can lead to data corruption
later.

Good luck!

