Malcolm Dunnett wrote:
> 
> In article <7i4en0$pos@usenet.pa.dec.com>,
>     hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.nospam (Hoff Hoffman) writes:
> >
> >   PowerStorm 3D30 or better...  Dual-head it, if you can swing it...
> >
> >   Systems here are capable of running multiple operating systems, as is
> >   the PowerStorm 3D30 controller.
> 
>      The PowerStorm 4D10T works on NT and VMS ( 7.2 with latest graphics
>      patch kit ). I find it nicer than the 3D30 ( better resolution, 24 bit
>      colour vs 8 bit colour on the 3D30 ). This is for typical "windows"
>      stuff, I don't know how the cards compare on 3D stuff.


The 3D30 (8-plane) and 4D20 (24-plane) TGA2 options are still among the
fastest 2D graphics options ever built, and the X11 server has quite a
few years of highly optimized coding.  The 4D10T (ELSA GLoria Synergy-8)
can't come close to the 3D30 or the 4D20 performance.

Having said that, the 4D10T is cheap, and like most PC options can be
configured for different depths (24 and 8 are supported on VMS).  It
will run up to 1920x1200 at 8-bits, and 1280x1024 at 24 bits.  No 3D. 
No overlay planes.  No Z-buffer.  No simultanious pixel
formats/colormaps/depths.  All of which the 4D20 has.



