In article <374110A2.67761C35@hda.hydro.com>,
	Terje Mathisen <Terje.Mathisen@hda.hydro.com> writes:
> PS. Almost all the really high-performance asm kernels I write tend to
> use base+index+offset addressing in the innermost loops:
> 
>   ESI -> just past the end of input array
>   EDI -> just past the end of output array
>   ECX = nr of elements (or size in bytes/size in unrolled blocks etc)
> 
> ; Unrolled 2-8 times:
> OFFSET = 0
> next4:
> REPT 4
>   mov eax,[esi + ecx * SCALE_FACTOR + OFFSET]
> ... Do something
>   mov [edi+ + ecx * SCALE_FACTOR + OFFSET]
> OFFSET = OFFSET + ELEMENT_SIZE
> ENDM
> 
> ; Update loop counter/address index & branch back
>   add esi, BLOCK_SIZE
>    jb next4
> 
> The individual iterations are of course mixed together for better
> pipeling, and the top of the loop will avoid any loop-counter based
> addressing which would cause AGIs, but otherwise that is the normal
> logic.
> 
> With just base or base + offset addresses, I would have needed more
> instructions to update the source & destination pointers as well.

Well, it's all a question of where you put your adders and
instruction/addressing mode bits, isn't it?

Thinking as I write, I can see that this way does have the advantage
of not requiring the incremented addresses to be written back to
the register file, but neglecting that factor, it doesn't seem to
be dramatically more efficient than post-increment addressing modes
on other processors.

-- 
Andrew Reilly



