Jonathan Stone wrote:
> 
> In article <slrn7k0vuj.hh.mike@ducky.net>, mike@ducky.net (Mike Haertel) writes:
> |> In article <7hp5if$11co$1@news.doit.wisc.edu>, Andy Glew wrote:
> |> >Actually, based pointers have absolutely no overhead
> |> >on processors with the appropriate addressing modes.
> |>
> |> You are conveniently overlooking the fact that providing
> |> the "appropriate addressing modes" may have circuit impact
> |> in a place where you'd rather not.
> 
> and plus extra register pressure, which may cause more spills, more
> cache misses, ...  ``Absolutely no overhead'' sounds awfully quantitative.
> Got numbers to back it up?

Andy is correct to a first order approximation, on recent x86 cpus, but
as noted the requirement for base+index+offset addressing has been a
sore point on many x86 implementations, i.e. it caused an extra pipeline
bubble on at least the 486 cpus.

The "real" problem with based pointer on x86 though isn't just the
register pressure, but the fact that it takes away the most advanced
addressing mode from the application itself, i.e. it is no longer
possible to directly address something like a specific element in an
array of structs, unless the array starts at the bottom of the based
segment itself (or at a fixed offset from the bottom).

OTOH, as long as we're going to use asm code, based pointers are
definitely easier (more natural) to use on x86 (or M68K) than on more
modern cpus with simpler (faster?) address arithmetic.

It will still be problematical to write stable & portable C code to
support the concept.

Terje

PS. Almost all the really high-performance asm kernels I write tend to
use base+index+offset addressing in the innermost loops:

  ESI -> just past the end of input array
  EDI -> just past the end of output array
  ECX = nr of elements (or size in bytes/size in unrolled blocks etc)

; Unrolled 2-8 times:
OFFSET = 0
next4:
REPT 4
  mov eax,[esi + ecx * SCALE_FACTOR + OFFSET]
... Do something
  mov [edi+ + ecx * SCALE_FACTOR + OFFSET]
OFFSET = OFFSET + ELEMENT_SIZE
ENDM

; Update loop counter/address index & branch back
  add esi, BLOCK_SIZE
   jb next4

The individual iterations are of course mixed together for better
pipeling, and the top of the loop will avoid any loop-counter based
addressing which would cause AGIs, but otherwise that is the normal
logic.

With just base or base + offset addresses, I would have needed more
instructions to update the source & destination pointers as well.

