sponk@my-dejanews.com wrote:
: I have some FORTRAN code that seems to be
: particularly greedy about reserving my swap space
: .. but then doesn't use the reserved space hardly
: at all.  This means that I can only run a couple
: processes at a time, which is extrememly annoying.

Basically, you have a choice of two options:

1. Switch to the lazy swap allocation mode. Simply remove
   /sbin/swapdefault, and reboot. The danger here is that
   if you run out of swap in the lazy mode, you can count
   on a spectacular system crash.

2. Go over your Fortran code, and replace large statically 
   dimentioned arrays with dynamically allocated arrays
   using as much space as needed. Depending on the internal
   sofistication of the program you use, this may be either
   extremely simple or a royal pain in the neck. YMMV.

2a. Sorry, there is no magic compiler switch which can do 
   this for you. May be if Compaq implements the rumoured
   DWIM instruction...

/Serge.P

