Nick Michell wrote:
> 
> 
> There are some interesting things in the complete Spec numbers for the
> PA8500 and 21264.  The 21264 has a much bigger difference between base
> and peak numbers, especially on floating point.  Some base numbers are
> only 1/2 of the peak number!  Is it compiler immaturity that the base
> is so low, or stupid compiler tricks that make the peak number so high?
> 
>                 440Mhz PA8500           500MHz 21264 w/4M L2
>                 N4000 - 1 CPU           ES40 - 1 CPU
> 
> SPECfp95        base    peak            base    peak
> 
> su2cor          29.0    29.2            17.1    32.1    <- !!!
> turb3d          40.6    40.6            27.9    52.5    <- !!!
> 

For su2cor, the base compile is apparently
	kf77 -arch ev6 -O5 -transform_loops -fkapargs='-ur=1'
while the peak run is 
	kf90 -arch ev6 -O3 -transform_loops -automatic -fkapargs=' -ag=abfg
-r=0 -nointerleave -ur2=260 '

For turb3d, base is
	kf77 -arch ev6 -O5 -transform_loops -fkapargs='-ur=1'
while peak is
	kf90 -arch ev6 -O5 -speculate all -automatic -fkapargs=' -ag=a -o=1
-ur2=760 -ur3=100 '

(From the ES40 1-CPU entry on www.spec.org)

So, apparently kf77 eeks out a better overall number for base
(where only one set of tuning is allowed for all benchmarks),
but turb3d and su2cor like the newer f90 compiler a lot more.



