In article <7ht2do$1oo$1@news.vic.com>, "Rob Cervantez" <rob@DONOTSPAM.symcas-tsg.com> writes:
>I've installed 2 VNSwitches (EF and XX) in the following configuration:
>
>clearVISN Multichassis Manager V6.3
>DECHub 900 MS, sw=V5.5
>VNSwitch900EF, sw=3.0-001 - 2 FDDI Modules in port 13
>VNSwitch900XX, sw=3.0-001 - 4 100Mb/s Ethernet (RJ45)

This are current versions...

>In Multichassis manager, I can see the VNSwitches, but the add-on modules
>are just gray in color.  When I double click one of the add-on modules (or
>the VNSwitch, for that matter), I get something to the effect of "MIB data
>not available (No Such)".  I would think that I simply need to get the
>latest MIB.. that's all fine and dandy, but how do I 'install' it?? I'm not
>sure if it goes in the Multichassis Manager application or what?

If it's gray, then it's not manageable. And the error message is every time
"MIB data not available (No Such)". That means the MCM has no info what MIB
to apply. So, no need to download a newer MIB or to install it in MCM !

The DEChub Mib files are required only for generic SNMP Managers just like
Cabletron SPECTRUM (here exists a better solution, too), TME, HP OpenView,
Sun Netmanager, DECmcc, ... The MCM has the MIB built in, and _if_ a newer
hub modules comes out, you need a newer MCM version (not in your case).

You have two ways to manage a module in a DEChub900: Direct or via the hub.

For direct management, the module has to have an IP config and SNMP
config (community). For indirect management, the module has to be
recognized by the hub and MCM has to use a modified SNMP config
(community-slot#). BUT: communication between DEChub900 and the module
is done via a 9600 Bd SLIP connection. This would be awfull slow if you
really want to manage all the manageable functions of a module (if you
use OBM you have the same performance problem, but OBM is used only in
rare cases).

So I recommend direct management every time. BUT for establishing direct
network communication to the module, the module must be initially
configured (besides IP and SNMP config: ports/segments redirected to the
backplane and connected to the appropriate backpanel segment). This
(limited function) can only be done via indirect communication and MCM
knows how to do it (Lan Interconnect Alt-L and then Config-Button).

It seems, that MCM started some versions ago, to use direct communication
even by the indirect management method, to speedup the management a lot.
But, if the direct communication is not available, then uses the indirect
communication via the hub SLIP port and all is/was well.

But it seems, that the last version(s) of MCM no longer tries to use the
indirect communication for all the functionality (at least for the newer
Hub modules) and only show(s) the modules greyed-out. I think, this is the
"problem" you have.

Add all modules to the MCM database (eg. via autoregistration) after this
modules got their own network config (Name, IP config, SNMP Config), check
the network communication (ping directly the modules) after hub segment
config and then try it again.

Good luck

-Peter

