Sander Vesik wrote:
> 
> In comp.arch Terje Mathisen <Terje.Mathisen@hda.hydro.com> wrote:
> > I considered that option, but it would require that all the possible
> > base addresses must be an integral multiple of the object size, which
> > would pretty much limit this approach to a small set of powers-of-two
> > object sizes.
> 
> How come? Most things in this world live pretty well with getting
> page aligned (is there really anything that gives you non-page aligned
> shared memory for something bigger than one page?) memory.
> 
> Any kind of shared memory will most probably be page aligned.

Agreed.

The problem is if I allocate an array of objects, where the object size
is relatively prime to 2, there is no way to access it from a different
base address with a simple base_index offset.

I.e. if I define

// Assume 32-bit pointers, so the record size will be 12 bytes
typedef struct _r { struct _r *next; struct _r *prev; float data; } r;

and then go on to allocate an array of these, where the pointers define
some internal order within the array

r a[SIZE]

then a second process which shares this buffer but mapped at a different
base address, can never calculate an index offset which would make the
following work:

  p = p->next + base_index;

This follows directly since 12 can never divide a power-of-two page size
(mmap address).

You always end up with the ugly casts back & forth to (char *).

Terje

