First, you need a SCSI host adapter such as the Adaptec 2940 family to
spin up the drive when the system is powered up.

Next, you need the jumper settings for the drive.  If one of the
jumper settings is to spin up the drive when power is first applied,
set the jumper and your problem is completely solved.

If the drive does not have a "spin up" jumper, go to
http://ftpsearch.lycos.com/?form=medium
and search for rzspinup*.* .  Then download the rzspinup program and
follow the instructions to set the drive to spin up... Ben Myers

