Bruce Hoult (bruce@hoult.actrix.gen.nz) wrote:

: I understand that some flavour of Unix is running on Palm Pilot now. 
: Whether Linux or BSD I don't recall.

: Since that's a 68000 with no MMU, in order to get fork() to work I expect
: they must have *had* to make all pointer dereferences based -- so that
: fork() copies the stack & heap elsewhere and just changes some designated
: An register.  Assuming that's how it is done, I can't imagine any
: practical way to do it other than through compiler support.

: Anyone knows different?

	I don't know about Palm/Unix, but have a problem with terms like
"must" :-) Minix on the Atari ST (A computer that, despite the name, had
nothing to do with the Atari _I_ work for :-) implemented fork() by
swapping, just as many early Unices did. Of course, they tried hard to
swap to memory, rather than drum, er... disk. They also tended to run the
child first, right after the fork, because odds were very good that it
would quickly exec(), mooting the swap. Ugly as this sounds, it actually
makes a certain amount of sense. Certainly more sense than expecting the
average Unix app to be sufficiently disciplined in its use of pointers
to allow based pointers to "just work" with a modified compiler. OTOH,
I seem to recall that the compiler for the Palm has 16-bit ints, but 32-bit
pointers. Perfectly legal for C, and arguably "the right thing to do" for
the 68000, but likely to make a whole lot of common code blow chunks.
Including at least one popular "portable" Smalltalk(tm) clone.

