How did you get such good SUN Enterprise numbers -- are you
using that new Version 2.0 "f90"?  Our experience with the
"f90" version 1.2 last fall were rather worse than this.

We picked benchmarks last fall, selected from our joint
project with Penn State to perform air quality forecasting
(see URL http://envpro.ncsc.org/projects/NAQP/), for both
MM5 and our MAQSIP air quality model.  Both benchmarks ran
just over 6 CPU-hours on PSU's (196 MHz R10K) Power
Challenge.  Our O-2000 (250 MHz R10K) runs them in just
over 5 cpu-hours.  One of our client's Digital 2100 (400Mhz
21164A) ran them in just over 4 CPU-hours.  One of our new
300 MHz Sun Enterprise servers runs MAQSIP in just over 4
hours as well -- but 29 hours for MM5!

This version of MAQSIP was re-coded to be friendly to
deeply pipelined microprocessor/parallel environments --
good registerization, cache-friendly data  structures,
avoiding parallel cache contention. Most of the work is
spent in the chemistry-solver (VERY stiff systems of ODE,
one cell at a time with its own internal time step, with
scratch variables that are either scalars or else small 1-D
chemical-species subscripted arrays) or the diffusion or
cloud solvers (scratch structures are 1-D vertical arrays
or are scalars).  Parallelism is via OpenMP Fortran.

MM5 is a vector-oriented code with sometimes-bad strides. 
I suspect a  combination of cache conflicts, TLB thrashing,
and poor register/cache utilization in the vector-style
code to be responsible.  Evidently the
Digital^H^H^H^H^H^H^HCompaq and SGI compilers can do
loop-rewriting, etc., that obviates these effects.


