In article <6JE_2.63$wq2.3845@nuq-read.news.verio.net>, "Eric Fish" <efish@ggsoftware.com> writes:
:Is there a way to automatically execute a script when logging on?

  Ayup, LOGIN.COM.

:Can I change the VT100 backspace key to "Backspace" from "Delete"?

  Assuming this is a real VT100 (wow, antique), the delete key is the
  delete key, and changing it is not easy.  If this is really DECwindows 
  (X Windows) or you are making remote access to the OpenVMS system from 
  a remote box, you can mess with the key mappings to make this change.
  (More information is required...)

:In a simple DCL, I tried to execute a series of CC commands.  I don't see
:the output from any of these on the screen.  Any clues as to why?

  Well, if you didn't ask for a display of output from the command
  procedure via SET VERIFY or f$verify(1), you won't see it...

:Is there a way to test, in a DCL command file, for the presence of a file,
:then conditionally execute something if the file exists?

  $ if f$search("SYS$LOGIN:LOGIN.COM") .eqs. "" then write sys$output "nope"

:Is there something along the lines of Unix's make for compiling and linking
:C programs?

  Yes, make and mms and similar are all available.  See the OpenVMS FAQ.
  MMS is a product, part of DECset.  Various versions of make are around, 
  including versions on the OpenVMS Freeware.  See the FAQ.

:Can I make references to files inside a VMS program with Unix-style
:directory references, such as "mydir/file1"?

  Assuming DEC C, yes, you can.  You may need to set up a logical name
  or two to help the C run-time figure out where certain stuff in the
  Unix file path is actually located, however.

