In article <926659080.114485@terra.ultra.net.au>, xenophon@starting-point.com writes:
>Greetings from distant Australia.  
>I have an old uVAX 3100/38 with VMS 5.5.2.
>Instead of a normal RS232 port it has the MMJ
>telephone type serial port. Now, can anyone tell
>me if it can be used to acccess the Net or am I
>flaggelating a deceased equine :-)
>Thankx, Darryl Georgee.

The MMJ is for console (the one with the 'printer' icon) and the other
is a serial port (the one with the 'two arrows' icon).

The uV3100s came with ethernet adapters and had connectivity via either
a 15 pin AUI/10base5 connector or a BNC thin-wire/cheapernet/10base2.
 
You could, conceivably, use the MMJ serial port with a modem to access
the "Net" via SLIP or PPP or some other protocol.  However, the serial
MMJ will not provide adequate modem control signals so, this would be
problematic.

You didn't mention if your particular uV3100 had any optional serial
connections.  If there are any, one of these would be a better choice
as it is likely that they would support/provide better modem control.

