In article <926659080.114485@terra.ultra.net.au>, xenophon@starting-point.com writes:
:Greetings from distant Australia.  
:I have an old uVAX 3100/38 with VMS 5.5.2.

  I will assume this is a VAXstation 3100 model 38 -- please be careful
  to avoid abbreviating system names.  In this case the system name is 
  rather clear, but that's definitely not always the case...

:Instead of a normal RS232 port it has the MMJ
:telephone type serial port. 

  The MMJ pinout is in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ),
  as are adapter part numbers.  With the appropriate home-made or 
  purchased adapter, this can be connected to most any other serial 
  port.  The OpeNVMS FAQ is available via a link at:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/

:Now, can anyone tell
:me if it can be used to acccess the Net or am I
:flaggelating a deceased equine :-)

  "Access the Net" isn't the most explicit of goals -- considerations
  include the sorts of protocol and operations required, the features
  available from your ISP, etc.  Point-to-point communications with 
  remote hosts are certainly possible with this system configuration.

  If you want to access a network that requires the installation of a
  client kit on your system, you are probably out of luck -- obtaining
  a client kit for OpenVMS will be difficult.  If on the other hand you	
  want a direct connection to an IP network, this can be configured...

  OpenVMS does not offer direct PPP connections on OpenVMS VAX, though 
  this is possible via a network connection to an external device such
  as a DECserver or external Ethernet-to-point-to-point IP router, and 
  from there out to the remote host.

  You will, of course, need an external modem.

  For the most common direct connection, see the SET HOST/DTE command
  and the SYS$EXAMPLES:DTE*.* stuff -- DTE_AT.C is around on recent
  systems, and allows SET HOST/DTE to communicate with a modem using
  the AT command set.  Otherwise, you can use SET HOST/DTE and enter
  the necessary modem dial-out commands directly.

  If this is a hobbyist system and DECUS in Australia offers it, you 
  can avail yourself of the DECUS OpenVMS hobbyist license, and move
  to something rather newer than OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2.  See the FAQ...

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




