In article <373C3D7E.51E11C05@isthq.com>, Daniel R Burk <dan@isthq.com> writes:
|> I'm in the same boat with my VaxStation 3100 M38.
|> 
|> My game plan is to get Motif on this machine, running OpenVMS 6.1 (or
|> 7.2 when the Hobbyist discs are ready), then
|> go find me a copy of Netscape 3.0 for Vax. I'll probably hook it via
|> ethernet connection to an old AMD 486/120 PC
|> running LINUX as a firewall to a cable modem.
|> 
|> No RS232 necessary, in this case.
|> 
|> Use of an external modem on the RS232 port is intriguing. Anyone out
|> there done this before?

No real modem control on the MMJ. And if it has a dc7085 or similar
dz11-on-a-chip clone, then it does interrupt-per-character.  The
performance of a cvax means its probably not worth trying.  The
hardware is likely not capable of higher speeds than 19200
(and even the nominal 19200 may acutally be 19600).

If you want a modem, put a free-unix on an i386, hook up a 16550 uart,
and hang the modem there.



