Sorry, the context got trimmed.  Just how much of the `flexibility' of
the single-address-space model being advocated does the AS/400
hardware and OSes acutally deliver, when compared to per-process
address spaces?  The references and experiences I'm familiar with
(which date back to System/38) seemd to indicate not much.

We can do SAS like the Mac or Windows does it, and lose any isolation
between processes. Or we can do it like hardware capability machines
with a segment (capability) per object, to prevent pointer-arithmetic
errors from unintentionally clobbering adjacent objects.  Or we can
pay all the penalties for using a nonwstandard architecture.  Or we
can do it in software and trust the compilers.  (Aren't AS/400s
largely compiler-based, with one extra tag bit to distinguish pointers?)

I'm still trying to understand just what `flexibility' gains Stefan
and others are after.  I'm still not sure precisely how much
`flexibility' is wanted, or just how much risk (from bad code in other
processes, etc) they're willing to accept to get it.



