Never seen a Starion 960 up close, but it should work like any other
Pentium class computer with graphics on the motherboard.  It needs no
jumper to disable the on-board graphics.  It simply senses the
presence of an add-in graphics card, and disables the on-board
graphics.

I thnbk that the issue you have encountered is the usual nasty
computer industry problem of creeping specifications.  I would guess
that the Starion 960 was designed to meet version 2.0 of the PCI
specification.  The Voodoo3 conforms to the later PCI 2.1 spec.  The
rule of thumb is that cards that comply with a later revision of a
spec may not work with a motherboard designed to an earlier spec.
Check with the Voodoo folks or the manual they supply.

The question is why in hell do the know-it-alls who design and
manufacture computer hardware do it do us all the time?  I'll bet that
PCI 2.1 is 0.00005% faster than PCI 2.0.  Don't these people realize
that there is a TREMENDOUS number, a hundred million maybe(?), of PCI
2.0 Pentium boxes with owners just aching to goose up the graphics
performance?  Sheesh!  How hard is it to design a card that works with
both PCI 2.0 and PCI 2.1?  Computer board manufacturers, don't be
bashful!  Please respond.

Off my soapbox... Ben Myers

