In article <371e231d.0@news.destek.net>,
Dave Cherkus <cherkus@unimaster.com> wrote:
>
>Can you discuss how the OS should use this?

  I'm not the person you replied to, but I do have an educated opinion..
  Obviously, it would be trivial for the OS to use the 64GB physical
memory space to leave individual processes with larger working sets,
and this would be a GoodThing[tm].

>Another posting said most UNIX kernels presume they can access the
>entire address space of one process at the same time they retain access
>to the kernel's code and data.  So, if the size of the kernel is K, the
>max size a process can be is 2^32-K.
>
>Are you expecting that presumption to change?

  It might.  There are plenty of people out there willing to do
something new.  The obvious solution would be for the kernel to
have some sort of interface for juggling page table records and
thus map different parts of a 4GB process space into the 4GB
kernel space.

>Or are you expecting the kernel to provide a programming interface
>where the process can treat part of its 2^32-K address space as a
>window into the 2^36 or greater address space?

  Heh :-) the DOS folks did this for years, to swap memory regions
between the normally inaccessible "high memory" and the 640k of us-
able "low memory".  It's certainly doable, but hardly pretty, and 
to be truly useful it would take a lot of co-operation from the 
compiler folks (which, in my experience, is often hard to come by).  
Alternatively the programming interface could be used explicitly in 
select standard library functions to implement things like selective
mapping of disk cache data into the user's process space, which 
would yield a kind of split memory organization where a process 
could have 4GB of stack, heap, and code, and up to 60GB of cached
file data (or another way of looking at it is memory that can only
be accessed through the file io library).

>Or are you presuming support for large processes isn't important,
>and the extra address space should be used to keep more 2^32-K-sized
>processes in memory at a time?

  This would be the obvious "easy" solution.  I'm not going to say
that large process support is or isn't important, because it really
depends on what you're using your computer to do.  Some applications
work best with large processes while others do fine (or actually work
better) as multiple processes.  Certainly the ability to grant multi-
ple 4GB-sized processes larger working sets can do a lot of people a
lot of good, but frankly I'd rather plop down $7K for a nice beefy
Alpha box and have the best of both worlds.  Unfortunately not all
companies can make that choice.  Sometimes they need to run software
which is only supported on x86, and sometimes they only have MIS staff
who are experienced with x86's and cannot justify the cost of hiring
more staff or training their existing staff, and sometimes everyone's
running haggard trying to make deadlines and simply can't afford to
do research or think too hard about solutions they have little know-
ledge of, so they go with what they know (ie, buy a big multi-Xeon
box).

  -- TTK




