In article <372A6EF1.793A8BB5@igs.net>, Paul DeMone  <pdemone@igs.net> wrote:
>
>   What is nonsense is you touting two architectures that have rarely
>   met the integer performance of x86s let alone compete with the RISC
>   big boys or potential IA-64 implementations.  These RISC also rans
>   couldn't make a decent speed bump in Intel's road to IA-64 hegemony.

  To inject a reality check:

  (1) SPARC has a sufficiently entrenched software and user base that 
SPARC implementations can remain commercially viable for a long time,
despite their poor per-unit performance.

  (2) One of the reasons companies purchase SPARC systems is so they 
can run the same software on engineers' desktop machines as they do on
their 24+ processor servers.  x86-based workstations may be better in 
terms of price and performance for the desktop, but since large x86 
systems have not been available at *all*, x86 hasn't been an appropriate 
solution to these companies' problems.

  The long and profitable life of the x86 architecture is testament to 
the inappropriateness of touting technical excellence (or the lack of 
it) as a causal factor in the viability of a processor family.

  In order to supplant the performance-poor SPARC, IA64 systems will
have to:
  (1) Have sufficient software support
  (2) Be available in low-end (1- or 2-processor) workstations for 
      less than $10,000
  (3) Be available in high-end (24- or 32-processor) servers and be 
      able to scale as well as existing SPARC systems.

  And from everything I've seen, this is what Intel seems to want 
to do with it.  We will see how they do.  Historically they have not
done very well with #3 -- even their Xeons, which supposedly target 
the server market, have not been available in configurations larger
than quad-processor until relatively recently.

  -- TTK




