  the TCP/IP Services (or other currently-installed IP package) as well.

:Existing application developed by C++. I am a
:Visual Basic (ver 5.0) programmer.

  You may find it advantageous to learn C++.

: I don't know much about protocols, firewall

  The Visual packages tend to shield the programmer from most of the
  really interesting stuff -- I'm not sure they actually make it easier 
  to get started, but they do guide you through the available Microsoft
  interfaces, extensions, and options.  (All the things that you need
  to avoid if portability is of interest.)

  Detailed discussions of protocols and firewalls are projects suited 
  to PhD thesis -- they are whole areas of specialties unto themselves.
  What you need to do is to start with the problem you need to solve,
  and work from there -- it is far to easy to get snowed under with all
  of the terminology and even the hype in the market.

  For information on the Enterprise Toolkit, see:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/commercial/cace.html

:I want to create a link from my Pentium to Mainframe.

  "Pentium to Mainframe" sounds so quaint -- as a suggestion, you will
  want to communicate from Microsoft Windows NT or Windows NT to your
  OpenVMS VAX or OpenvMS Alpha system.

  You will also want to consider this as a client-server or a peer-to-peer
  configuration, and work from there -- you will need to establish a set
  of available protocols that are available on all platforms, and then
  implement your applications on those protocols.  If you avoid embedding
  dependence on the platform-specific cruft -- this can be difficult when
  stuck in Visual environments -- and if you can keep it to a subset of 
  your source modules, then you can generally port your source code around
  freely.

: In Pentium I got
:Windows-95 & Microsoft Visual Basic 5.0 Ent. Verson. Is that any Active-X
:control which links both hardware as well as software?

  OpenVMS V7.2 supports the Microsoft COM environment, as well as DCE RPC
  and other similar communications protocols.

:� What are the possibilities of hardware connect (i.e. ports) which supports
:both Pentium & Mainframe?

  Marketing aside, only the operating system and operating system tools and
  compilers will run on and will make direct use of most features of the 
  hardware.  (You generally won't find IA86, VAX, or Alpha register 
  references or embedded instructions in your BASIC code, the compiler(s)
  and operating system tools take care of that for you.  All but a very 
  select and rare group of applications will operate on and depend on the 
  operating system and the compilers -- thus the hardware is arguably 
  secondary to the operating system when considering interoperation and
  portability.

:� What are the possibilities of Software Connect (i.e. protocols/ RCP)?

  DECnet, TCP/IP, UDP/IP, SMTP, SNMP, RPC, Microsoft COM, X Windows, etc.

  Recent OpenVMS releases also offer an optional Win32 API from Bristol
  Technologies.

:� What are the possibilities of send data formats from local machine (Pemtium)
:to Remote machine (Mainframe)?

  There are many possibilities -- floating point, ASCII, UNICODE, and
  a large variety of other formats are available and can be interchanged.

:� If VB Winsock Active Control with TCP or UDP protocols solves the above
:problem how the send a user input as packet format from Pentium to Mainframe?

  What you should send depends highly on what the problem is.

:� Which is best protocol for above problem. Is it TCP or UDP?

  Again, the best protocol depends on the particular problem.  Focus
  first on the problem, and then later on the tools.

:� Is Winsock supports to create a Packet? If No, how to link TCP program into
:VB application?

  Please ask one of the Microsoft folks -- from C, my assumption is that 
  you would avoid the Microsoft-proprietary interfaces and the Microsoft 
  extensions and use the standard C interface to TCP/IP -- the C socket 
  library.  As for Visual BASIC, I am not familiar with it nor with how
  it interfaces with IP.

:� What will be the Server side program, which Listen an incoming Client
:Request? Can we use VB5.0 application in Mainframe?

  While it is possible to port BASIC applications around, BASIC tends 
  to be rather less commonly ported around than some other languages.

:� After received the data from Remote machine (Mainframe) how to decode
:Packets to show results on my VB application? ******* � Any the possibilities
:of above problem solved by another software and Hardware?

  BEA MessageQ and various middleware solutions are available.

  ODBC access to RMS and to OpenVMS databases are available, for RMS
  access see the ISG Navigator package.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




