> But how would you get *enough* security (say, to cope with wild

Look at it this way:  take a simplistic Unix system where each process' address
space is a single contiguous block starting at 0.  Now move each one of those
processes within their address space so that they occupy non-overlapping
(virtual) addresses.  They still can't access any other memory but their own
(they each have a separate protection domain).
Yes, this doesn't work too well with fork(), but it's not too bad.
Furthermore, pointers can now be exchanged between processes in a meaningful
manner.  SHM segments are now always mapped at the same address in each process
which allows the use of actual data-structures in there.

