From: fairfield@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu
Subject: [Sum] How to create a bootable (TK70) diagnostics tape (VAX)?
Date: 1999/05/11
Message-ID: <1999May11.191147.1@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 476834408
Organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms

        Last week I  asked  how  to  create  what  I  called a "bootable
    diagnostics  tape" on a TK70 in order to boot a VAX 6620 and  update
    its CIXCD firmware (my FSE says that should  be  called  a  "console
    tape").  I'm happy to report success!

        First, thanks  to  all  those  who  replied  here  or privately,
    including   Jonathan  Stone,  Antonio  Carlini,  George  Cook,   but
    especially Steve Hoffman who gave a correct order  that  the  system
    would need to find the files on the tape.

        Here's what I did (what  I  _should_  have done is noted later):
    With the CDrom mounted to VMS (DAD10:), and a blank cartridge in the
    TK70 (MUA5:),

        $ init mua5: cons_w
        $ mount /block=512 mua5: cons_w
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]vmb.exe mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]diagboot.exe mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]elsaa.exe mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]cibca.bin mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]cixcd.bin mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]evsba.exe mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]e*.exe/excl=(elsaa,evsba) mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]*.bin/excl=(cibca,cixcd) mua5:
        $ copy dad10:[sys0.sysmaint]*.hlp mua5:
        $ dismount/nounload mua5:

        Having done this, and  with  the  system subsequently shut down,
    booting  from  the  tape with an appropriate boot command  gets  you
    directly into the diagnostics  supervisor,  DS>  prompt.  :-)   From
    there you do whatever you _would_ have done after booting the CDROM,
    but  _much_ more _slowly_!  It took a full hour, elapsed time, to do
    the CIXCD firmware upgrade!  This takes  all of about 5 minutes when
    booting from the Infoserver...

        Now the things that were  done  less than optimally were, first,
    copying  files  that weren't needed for _this_ upgrade to the  tape.
    All that were really needed are  VMB.EXE,  DIAGBOOT.EXE,  ELSAA.EXE,
    EVGEB.EXE  and  CIXCD.BIN.  Other devices would require other files.
    Secondly, the _placement_ of  the  files  on  tape was poor for this
    upgrade.    I  should  have  placed  EVGEB.EXE  directly   following
    ELSAA.EXE, and CIXCD.BIN following that.   _Presumably_,  even  with
    all  the other files copied to tape following these, this would have
    prevented the need to  read  the  tape  all  the  way to the end and
    rewind   it  _three_  times  (our  guess-timate  from  (im)patiently
    watching the TK70's blinkenlights flash).

        BTW, I determined  that  we  needed  to  run  EVGEB.EXE  to load
    CIXCD.BIN  by searching the *.hlp files for "CIXCD".  That shows  up
    only in ELSAA.HLP and EVGEB.HLP (on this  CD),  IIRC,  which  pretty
    much  pins  it  down  to  EVGEB.  A similar strategy should work for
    other devices.  [CSC had told me  I  needed to use EVGEA.EXE to load
    CIXCD.BIN, but there is no such file on this CD.]

        Finally, thanks to those who pointed out that I _should_ be able
    to MOP boot the VAX 6620 from another VMS host.  There were too many
    uncertainties with a  limited  down  time  to  allow  me to try that
    approach.   However,  if I ever do this again, I intend to create  a
    temporary partition and associated service on the Infoserver's  hard
    disk  (if  I can), and duplicate the CDrom there with, in this case,
    the one file in question replaced by what I was sent by CSC.

        Again, thanks for all the pointers!

            -Ken
