The 440 MHz PA-8500 has now taken over the SPECint95 lead from the
575 MHz 21264.  But the 500 MHz 21264 in the DS20 still retains the SPECfp95
crown.  This is the reverse of the 21164 vs PA-8000/8200
contest where Alpha had the integer advantage and PA-RISC usually
held a slight FP lead.

The uniprocessor performance battle now shapes up like:

  System       CPU     Clock,MHz  SPECint95  SPECfp95

 N4000       PA-8500      440       34.0       51.4
 8400 6/575   21264       575       30.3       47.7
 DS20         21264       500       27.7       58.7
 ES40         21264       500       27.3       57.7
 9000 C360   PA-8500      367       26.0       28.1
 MS440GX     PIII Xeon    550       23.6       16.9


According to the historical script played out over many previous
generations of Alphas and PA-RISCs, Comdeq will within days release
the minor shrink of the 21264 to 0.28 um known as EV67.  A 667 MHz
EV67 should be good enough to grab back the SPECint95 crown.

It's like the RISC version of the 49ers vs the cowboys  :)

BTW, minor note: Merced supposedly tapes out in two weeks;
place your bets now - win, place or show?


All opinions strictly my own.
--
Paul W. DeMone       The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race of EPIC
Kanata, Ontario      proportions to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com    architectures with MIPSed results but ALPHA's well
pdemone@igs.net      that ends well.



