Don Dossa <dossa1@llnl.gov> writes:

> The claim that Alpha is the fastest CPU for only the SPEC codes is
> simply not true.  And the world is much larger than TPC-C.  An example
> of a significant benchmark outside of SPEC and transaction processing is
> the MM5 mesoscopic meteorology program at Penn State and NCAR.
> (http://www.mmm.ucar.edu/mm5/mm5-home.html)
> A list of run times for a specific, interesting, and relevant model from
> the major vendors shows that DEC has the 4 fastest systems.

Oh, please.  While I don't doubt the published numbers, you should
have mentioned: 1 CPU, 16MByte memory.  Add the fact that the recent
Sun numbers weren't done by the same people and that both the "Sun
Fujitsu HALstation 300" [sic!] and the Ultra1 results look extremely
fishy (g77 or even f2c results?).  There is no mention of the
configuration and the compilers used for most of the systems.

Every large DEC Alpha system I know of (only a handful, I should say)
has disappointed the hopes of it's users at least initially.  The
equation

runtime_new = runtime old * (SPECfp_old / SPECfp_new)

simply does not hold for any system (or only by accident), but for
Alpha it has been out by far more than what people have been used to.
Also, all of these systems are now run solely through batch systems,
because they simply don't behave well if the load surpasses a certain
threshold.  I have no beef with that, I just note that the users of
these systems did not expect that they would have to do that.  The
learning curve and the related unexpected expenses have been painful
in all these cases.


