Sander Vesik wrote:
> 
> In comp.arch "Stefan Monnier " <foo@acm.commonnier+comp/arch/news/@tequila.cs.yale.edu> wrote:
> > Furthermore, pointers can now be exchanged between processes in a meaningful
> > manner.  SHM segments are now always mapped at the same address in each process
> > which allows the use of actual data-structures in there.
> 
> You can use actual data structures in shared memory even now (at least in
> mmap shared memory). You just have to use offsets relative to the start
> of the shared region instead of an absolute address.
> 
> Considering the cost of following a pointer, the cost of one addition to
> get the pointer which to follow is so insignificant as to not be worth
> mentioning.

This is right and wrong:

Yes, since even the PII/PII which has a very short load-to-use latency
needs 3 cycles per pointer to follow a chain (in L1 cache), the extra
addition can never add more than 33% to the running time, and as long as
you're actually doing something with the pointers, much less.

OTOH, it makes it much harder to write error-free high-level code that
uses these data structures, languages which simply don't allow direct
access to pointers need not apply at all.

(Is this true? Can you make based pointers work in Java? What about
Ada?)

Terje

PS. Of course, coming from an x86 background with near, far, based, huge
and flat addresses, a simple two-part address doesn't look too
intimidating, but it still seems similar to the double-linked list with
a single pointer trick, using XOR'ed prev/next links.

