Terje Mathisen (Terje.Mathisen@hda.hydro.com) wrote:
: Sander Vesik wrote:
: > Searching in a linked list would be:
: > 
: >         while (k != SHARED_NULL)
: >                 {
: >                 if (k->data != item)
: >                         k=(struct xyz*)((char *)(k->next) + seg_base);

: I believe most programmers will agree that the line above is a _lot_
: more likely to cause bugs than a (type-safe) 

: 	k = k->next;

Imagine a function that is accessing objects in two different shared
segments. (After all, we want at least moderately fine grained sharing
right?) In which case, that seg_base global no longer works. You need N of
them and some way to manage them, etc. The PL/I mavens know the problem
well. (PL/i allowed static declarations associating bases with pointers. I
am told it was common enough for bugs to creep in by getting the base
association for a pointer wrong.)

But the worse problem is that if you had a standard library routine to
search a linked list, you could not use it given the above. Compiler
support cannot easily solve this as base address management will have to
be known to the compiler. Also, it is doubtful that people will accept the
performance hit readily enough to compile all code this way.

-Z-



