In article <sorrynospam-0505992148090001@a35-15.chorus.net>, sorrynospam@spam.com (Eeeek! the Cat) writes:
:I just got an old DEC Vaxstation 3100 - Model VS42A-BW. Has two hard
:drives inside & a floppy drive. Don't have any keyboard, mouse or monitor
:for it. But it powers up and was wondering what resolution & scan rate of
:monitor it needed - and can I use a third party monitor? Has a "8-plane"
:graphics card.

  The key piece of hardware is the monitor cable -- that can be the
  toughest part to find, and the easiest to loose...

  The key monitor concept for older VAXstation graphics is a requirement
  for is "synch-on-green" signalling -- almost all of these older VAXstation 
  systems tend to use synch-on-green RGB monitors -- three BNC connectors.

    VAXstation        Ctrlr  Cable        Example Monitors
    ----------        -----  -----        ----------------
    VS3100 Monochrome  GPX   BC23K        VR260, VR262, VR150
    VS3100 Monochrome  GPX   BC18P-10     VR260, VR262, VR150
    VS3100 Greyscale   GPX   BC19S        VR260, VR262, VR150
    VS3100 Greyscale   SPX   BC23J-03     VR260, VR262, VR150, VR319
    VS3100 Color       GPX   BC23J-03     VR290, VR299, VR297, VR160
    VS3100 Color       GPX   BC19S        VR290, VR299, VR297, VR160
    VS3100 Color       SPX   BC23J-03     VR290, VR299, VR297, VR160

  Greyscale displays require a color co-processor board.  The SPX graphics
  controller is switchable between 60 Hz 1024 x 864 and 66 Hz 1280 x 1024,
  while the GPX uses 60 Hz 1024 x 864.  Both use RGB synch-on-green...

  Also see the OpenVMS FAQ for pointers to various hardware-oriented
  websites around the 'net and a variety of other information...  The
  site http://www.openvms.digital.com/ has a link to the OpenVMS FAQ.

:Please send a reply to: smilncat@terracom.net

  Ask here, get an answer here.

