The Alpha compilers/linkers that you are running may take up a lot of memory if
they are running with full optimization. There should be a compiler option to
enable/disable in-lining. In-lining is an option that takes the loop out of
loops, laying out a loop in one continuous stream of code. This eliminates
inefficient branch instructions, but takes up a lot more space and memory.
Check the vitrual memory allocation for the process. It may be very high. Check
also for additional processes, which may also use lots of memory, if you are
using make. I usually use VMS on Alpha and have only recently started Unix, so
I don't know the details in Unix, but the above is true on VMS, so it's
probably true on DEC Unix. Make sure the inline switch is off unless you cannot
afford anything less than the best performance all the time. I don't think that
128MB sounds like much for a swap file if you have multiple process using lots
of VM. Your page fault count looks very high. You could be paging to your swap
file. This could be the result of allocating too little memory to a process,
resulting in heavy page/swap file usage.



