Subject: Re: What's the difference between SPX and GPX
Date: 1999/05/07
Message-ID: <7gum01$4u5$1@news.news.kodak.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 475134774
References: <7gpo5d$m8a$1@news00.btx.dtag.de> <11AEA766E38CBCC1.DDFD53B8C79A2A82.B6E5193D124E4209@library-proxy.airnews.net>
Organization: Eastman Kodak Company
X-MimeOLE: Produced By Microsoft MimeOLE V4.72.3110.3
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

The GPX chipset was initially designed to go into a terminal, not a
workstation.  It started its life as a workstation in the color VAXstation
systems based on the Q-bus MicroVAX II (VAXstation II/GPX) and the
VAXstation 3500 products.  The first GPX was introduced in (I think) 1985 or
so and continued as the primary color graphics subsystem (with a different
board layout and interface) when the VAXstation 3100 was introduced in March
1989.  The GPX was available in both 4-plane (16 colors) and 8-plane (256
colors) versions although the 4-plane board was primarily used in grayscale
systems and not color systems. (I don't think the 4-plane GPX for VS3100
ever shipped to customers, I may be wrong)  the GPX boards supported the
1024x864 resolution format only.


the SPX chipset was designed only for the VAXstation line of products and is
significantly faster than the GPX chipset.  It was introduced after the
VAXstation 3100 Model 38/40 was introduced but prior to the introduction of
the VAXstation 3100 Model 76. (I think it was introduced some time in 1990)
and continued on as the primary color option for the Model 76. The SPX
supported both 1024x864 and 1280x1024 via switch selection on the board.
(also, the SPX was never made as a Q-bus board, only the VAXstation 3100
format)

The SPX chipset was also used in the 3D accelerators on the VAXstation 4000
line (except for the VLC) in the form of the SPXg (SPX with geometry
acceleration) and SPXgt  (SPX with geometry acceleration and true-color
(24-plane))  The VAXstation 4000 had its 2D accelerator in the system
interface chip, also known as LCG (Low Cost Graphics)

One final note, the UIS (or VWS if you care) software was never ported to
the SPX board, it was supported only by DECwindows.


