"Paul S. Brown" wrote:
> 
> Stephen W. Hodge <shodge@opus.freac.fsu.edu> wrote:
> > I just loaded SAMBA on my Alphastation.  I can see the machine from my Intel
> > NT machines with no problem.  When I double-click on the machine I get a
> > "incorrect password or unknown username" message.  It then asks me to supply
> > a "connect as" (I am assuming this is the username) and password.
> 
> > After I supply a valid username and password I get an error message that
> > says:
> 
> >     The account is not authorized to login from this station.
> 
> > I have the NT machine in the hosts file and in the host.equiv file on the
> > alphastation.
> 
> > Any ideas as to what I'm doing wrong?
> 
> Yes.
> 
> Sorry, seriously SAMBA maintains it's own password file completely separate
> from the UNIX passwd system. On my machine this lives in
> 
> /usr/local/samba/private/smbpasswd
> 
> There are some utilities (smbpasswd, addtosmbpass) which are used for
> maintaining this file. There is also a mksmbpasswd.sh included in the
> source distro for Samba which can be used to convert your UNIX passwd
> file to smbpasswd.

In addition, this "problem" cropped up with Win NT sp3, which changed
the way NT sends passwords.  Specifically, they're now encrypted by 
default.  The Samba web page has instructions on how to either add
the smbpasswd file, as Paul suggests, or to disable the password 
encryption on Win NT.  I think the former idea of adding the smpasswd
file is a better one.

Jeff



