In article <7gqnuu$br4$1@cantuc.canterbury.ac.nz>, E.Havelaar@Regy.Canterbury.Ac.NZ (Ed Havelaar) writes:
> I was having a little trouble creating a file with a script:
> 
> 
> 
> $      pid == f$getjpi("","PID")
> $
> $      def data_out "$$campwork:intl_''pid'.dat"
> $
> $CREATE DATA_OUT
> HI THERE
> $type data_out
> %TYPE-W-SEARCHFAIL, error searching for UOC$PIGGY:[CAMPUS.WORK]INTL_2240066F.DAT;
> -RMS-E-FNF, file not found
> $EXIT
> 
> 
> 
> Okay, fine I thought -- I have turned the error messages off.
                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

        This is a very strange statement, IMHO.  Why in the world do you
    turn off error messages?!  And if  you _really_ had turned them off,
    how  in  the world would you have gotten the  %TYPE-W-SEARCHFAIL,...
    message??

        In any  case,  your  DCL  indicates  a  small  confusion between
    symbols  and  logical  names.  Were I to write the above,  I'd  make
    `pid' a _local_ symbol (use  a  single  equals  sign),  rather  than
    global  (double  equals  sign), or more likely, I'd avoid creating a
    separate symbol altogether.  And  when referencing something through
    a logical name, I always make it a practice to include the colon for
    readability/understandability.  Like so:

----------------------------------------------------------------
$ Define DATA_OUT $$CAMPWORK:INTL_'F$GetJpi("","PID")'.DAT
$
$ Create DATA_OUT:
Some text
$
$ Type DATA_OUT:
$ Exit
----------------------------------------------------------------

> Let's see what happens when I do a SET MESSAGE...

        I seriously doubt the SET  MESSAGE  has  anything to do with it.
    I'd  check  the the definition of $$CAMPWORK, and your  disk  quota,
    etc.  My guess is that "something" changed between your  two  tests,
    like  you  cleaned up some files or something.  My guess is that the
    first test failed to  create  the  file  at all because of something
    _unrelated_  to  the command file, but if/since you  _had_  messages
    turned off, you had no indication of what that was!

        Stop screwing around with SET MESSAGE and you'll learn more
    quickly. :-)

        -Ken
