Yes they do but in this instance the Compaq/Digital would 
be the first to admit that the Wildfire boxes are very, very
late.

It is also terribly obvious that Digital didn't exactly invest 
in new mid range and high end server development. The GS60/8200 
and the GS140/8400 are old designs and it shows. They have 
by modern server standards slow I/O very limited backplane 
throughput. They don't exactly bristle with RAS facilities. As
a single box the Compaq Proliant is better configured in this 
respect and they compare very badly with other mid-high end 
UNIX systems. You can of course say that this is because 
they are old designs being compared with modern systems. But 
the story was the same 2 years ago when you compared 8400/8200s
with the systems competitors were shipping 2 years ago. 

Given the age of the 8400 and 8200 one could ask why they 
were not replaced or at least re-engineered a lot earlier, 
if they had then the hiatus while Compaq waits for Wildfire 
would not be so obvious.

> I am pleased that you actually specified "commercial OLTP workloads"
> in a recent performance posting, but the rest of your postings are
> still a bit Greek to me. If I didn't know better I would think you
> were just harping on anything that makes the competition look bad.
>

No actually I am responding in case you missed it to a posting
which was pure advocacy trying to make Sun and IBM look bad.

In doing so I should be pointing out the obvious to anyone 
interested in systems architectures, namely that a system is
a balance of CPU, Memory Hierachy, I/O, OS etc.


The type of client I work for is interested mainly in medium
to large servers that can handle commercial workloads with 
a small number os large HPC systems for things like simulation 
and scheduling. 

This discussion has attempted to define the success of in particular
the Alpha CPU in terms of SPECint_95 and SPECfp_95 while ignoring
the unpleasant fact that this is the only measure that shows 
Compaq in a good light. This is pure advocacy since everyone knows 
that there is not necessarely a relationship between SPECint/fp and 
actual application performance. 

Of course anyone taking the incredably narrow definition of systems 
performance as measured by SPECint/fp does so in the knowledge 
that choosing almost any other public benchmark shows a totally 
different story for Alpha.

Single system SPECrate_int and single and clustered SPECrate_fp,
TPC-C, TPC-D, NotesBench, SPECweb, SPECsfs, SAP/SD, BaaN BRU, Linpack
CDRS, DX, etc all tell a completely different story with no Alpha
advantage 
to be seen. Why, well to paraphrase Bill Clinton its the system
stupid.


