>> Qt and Be's libraries suffer from this.  I imagine any C++-based
>> library will.

> But without separating implementation from interface, how do you do
> this?

You don't.

> Sure, such separation is generally a good thing, only it costs. 

Virtual methods are also a good thing, but they cost, so C++
made them optional, so perhaps it should be an optional feature
of a class.  Something like

  class interface C {
  ...
  }

which signals to the compiler that this class is to be used
in the externally visible interface in a DLL and binary
compatibility across versions is desired.  That probably
means moving all private data members into a separate
autoallocated indirected area and prohibiting public and
protected data members.  Then you would issue errors for
inlined and non-virtual methods, and use a hashed lookup for
virtual methods to make sure the right one gets called even if
a later implementation reorders or adds methods (even adding is
dangerous if the class is subclassed with the present system).
Perhaps the run-time linker can do something clever and
efficient instead here.

Even a more minimal feature like:

  class padded 256 vmt 32 C {
  ...
  }

that automatically padded my sizeof up to 256, padded the
virtual method table to 32 and warned me if I exceeded those
limits would make the pad-your-objects-presciently method more
manageable without having to do horrendous manipulations with
sizeof and macros.

> C++ classes can efficiently represent quite small things - when you add

That's great, but not usually for the interface of a DLL.

> the level of indirection necessary to separate interface from
> implementation then you get "proper OO", but you need coarse granularity
> of objects to run at the same speed.  C++ was never intended to do what
> you're asking of it - if you want that, use another language.

What you are saying is that C++ is unsuitable for implementing
DLLs or shared libraries.  That's almost true, but that's a huge
area to cut it off from.

This isn't some niche application, shared libraries are everywhere.
Breaking each time a private data member is added is not an option.

> If you want speed, use C++

Ah, but what if I want speed, robustness and O-O in my shared
library/DLL?  Is C++ the wrong language, or do I have to be
incredibly disciplined and foresightful and cannot rely on
the compiler telling me when I screw up.

-- 
Erik Corry erik@arbat.com



