In article <7gap3k$d6r$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>,  <gfader@usa.net> wrote:
>I 'rescued' an old Application Dec 433MP server from the community
>spring clean-up here.  The machine appears to be complete, and contains
>one processor module, two memory modules, hard drive, cd-rom, floppy, etc.
>
>The machine boots up fine, and will run programs etc.  My question concerns
>the memory installed in it.  Each of the two memory modules is full (20 1 meg
>simms in each).  During the POST however, the machine only recognizes the
>first 7168k of extended ram.

Note:  Each memory module only has 16MB memory.  The other simms are for ECC.
I'm not sure why the POST isn't seeing 32MB unless some is bad.

You don't have the terminal mux board?  That uses some memory locations
and could be messing things up.

>I've tried all different combinations of simms in each board, but get the
>same result each time.	I've also downloaded the ECU program from Digital's
>website, but that had no effect as well.  I am able to enter the bios setup
>and key in the amount of extended ram, but that doesnt work either.
>
>I do see some dipswitches on the processor board, and its companion board.
>Without having a manual though, I have no idea what these are for.  I've tried
>flipping them in various combinations, but they dont seem to do anything.
>
>How do I get the machine to recognize that it has 32 megs of ram installed?

Even if you get the machine to recognize 32MB, the only OS I'm aware of
which can use more than 16MB is SCO UNIX.  The drivers were supposed to
have been folded into the standard distribution (which is now available
for a nominal fee for non commercial usage) though they were only available
from Digital when I last had a 433MP to play with in 1994.

Sam





