>The DecServer 900TM is a module which requires a means of powering it and 
>connecting it to the network. There are two choices: A Hub One, which fits 
>a single module, or a Hub900 which will take up to 8.

DECserver 900TM has 32 serial ports.
DECserver 90M (and the DECserver 90TL) has 8 ports.

You can have 8 DECserver 90M in a DEChub 90,
but you can't have 8 DECserver 900TM in a DEChub 900.

>The Hub One will need a transceiver for the type of media you use, probably
>10BASET or 10BASE2 if I had to guess. Either buy the Dec ones, which will
>fit the restricted space, or buy the smallest you can find and hope they 
>will fit.

eg. Allied Telesyn

>The Hub 900 will require a repeater in one of its 8 slots to run the network 
>connection - we usually use Repeater 90FA for this. It comes with one PSU
>and can take up to 3 more. One will run quite a lot of modules (usually more 
>than 4) but a second one gives redundancy. Price I think is around $US3000,
>so only worth it if you are using high densities of connections.
>
>You may find using several DecServer 90M modules in stack adapters works out
>cheaper. They have a built-in 10BASE2 connector. The Decserver literature
>raves on about it being an Access Server (RAS etc) and omits to mention
>that it is also a bog standard terminal server. It needs a load host,
>whereas the 900TM is standalone I believe.

The DECserver 90M has a Flash inside, so can be standalone.
The DECserver 900TM has a slot for a flash memory outside. If you provide
the flash card, it could run as a standalone module. Standard is not.

btw the DECserver 90M has a UTP Ethernet port on the front panel, too.
This, the flash memory and more RAM (you are able to run the Network
Access Software, which runs also in the DECserver 900TM) together make
the difference to the predecessor, the DECserver 90TL (4MB instead of 1MB).

>The load host can be a PC with Bootp, or a Vax or Alpha with MOP.

btw. I so far never managed to get my DECserver 90TL to boot via BOOTP,
though it is said to work...
