John Laird wrote in message ...

>> How big is the RZ25?  I couldn't find any information on it on the web.

There was an RZ25 which was 426MB (I still have two of them in my basement
PC server) and the RZ25L which was closer to 500MB.  The RZ2x series were
the "half height" 3.5 inch SCSI disks, the "L" variant were the 1" high
ones. Additionally, you need the "L" variants to fit INSIDE the VLC, only
the 1" high versions will fit.

BTW, there was an RZ22, which was 52MB, but it's rare.  It was used on lots
of VS3100 protos inside of DEC as well as sold in the "dataless"
configurations as page/swap disks.  Not many of them sold though.


So, you had RZ22 (52), RZ23 (104), RZ24 (210), RZ25 (426), RZ26 (1GB) and
then the RZ23L (123), RZ24L (224 I think) and RZ25L (about 500MB).   Most of
these came from Connor peripherals, but I think the RZ25 came from Quantum.
After these, DEC started making their own disks and used those for the
follow-on devices.  (this is about when I left DEC and stopped paying
attention to this stuff)
>
>Between 400Mb and 500Mb, depending on model.
>
>>> After much research (trust me), I identified 3 different graphics cards.
>>> Unless someone has been swapping bits around, the model identification
on
>>> the back is a guide to what was factory installed.
>>>
>>> VS48K-AA - 1024x768, 66(72?)Hz - RGB  (unknown connector, possibly
15-pin D)
>>> VS48K-BA - 1024x768, 60Hz      - SVGA (miniature D, PC-style)
>>> VS48K-CA - 1280x1024, 72Hz     - RGB  (DEC-special, 3 mini-coax)
>>>
>>> (I'm a little unsure about the first, as we've not got one in the
office)
>>> The -BA version was typically supplied with a 13" monitor (DEC used to
quote
>>> viewable area), any decent 14/15" device will do.

The 13" monitor sold with the -BA was a Sony Trinitron (I forget the part
number) that was re-badged for Digital.  It was an off-the-shelf monitor
though with the standard PC D-sub connector.  Any multisync should work with
this version.

If I remember correctly (I could look it up, I have a Configuration Guide at
home) the -AA was built to support the older 1024x864 VR2xx monitors.  I
don't know if DEC ever sold this, but the cards were designed just in case.
It had a 15-pin D-sub connector on it which was the same as the ones in the
VS3100's.

The -CA drove the high-res, DEC-variant of the Sony trinitron and DEC
internal 1280x1024 monitors.  Special cable with the mini-coax inside of a
D-Sub connector.







