In article <1999May4.131229.1@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu>, fairfield@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu writes:
:        Any help or hints would be  greatly appreciated!  I have a CIXCD
:    whose  firmware needs upgrading with a narrow window of  opportunity
:    (for downtime) rapidly approaching.

  Would using MOP be an option here?

  Make sure you have MOP service enabled via DECnet or (V6.2 or later)
  LANCP.  Here are the DECnet MOP commands for the MOP boot server host:

  $ RUN SYS$SSYTEM:NCP
  NCP> SHOW KNOWN CIRCUIT CHARACTERISTICS

  If MOP service is disabled, then enter these commands to enable it:

  NCP> SET CIRCUIT net-0 STATE OFF
  NCP> SET CIRCUIT net-0 SERVICE ENABLE
  NCP> SET CIRCUIT net-0 STATE ON

  Where net-0 is probably either EXA-0 or FXA-0, for DEMNA or DEMFA.
  (Using a command procedure for these three NCP commands can avoid 
  having DECnet circuits drop while the circuit state is set to off.)

  Now put the CIXCD microcode .SYS file into the directory MOM$SYSTEM:
  on the boot server.

  At the VAX 7000 series console, use a console command such as:

  >>> BOOT net0: -FILE name.SYS

  where net0: is probably either EXA0: or FXA0:, for the DEMNA or the 
  DEMFA controller, and name.SYS is the name of the firmware .SYS file.
  (eg: CIXCD_XEB.SYS)

  You should now find yourself running LFU...

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




