Hi,
At work I use a few different isdn routers, and they all seem to do just
what you want, 3-com cisco, most of the networking boys have one. Means you
need a household network but hey you wanted that.

Another option is to grab an old 3/486 with a network card and an isdn TA of
some sort (external usually easier to get software support for), install one
of the free unicies, in a minimal config, then set it up as a dial on demand
router. The linux howto project has lots of docs on setting up a linux box
as a router/firewall/dial-on-demand server/mail server/news server/http
proxy.../anything else. I guess other free unicies will provide similar
functionality.

This should get you started, and should work just about anywhere you can get
an isdn line, which in Germany shouldn't be a problem.

If you want to set the machine up for outside access (i.e. connect to it
from anywhere on the net), then you will need a leased line or a special
from your service provider, you may be able to get them to set up something
to dial you when someone tries to connect, but I doubt it.


Hope this helps, and don't let my mention of linux frighten you off, get the
docs have a look, if it's not what you want, well the advice is worth what
you paid for it, if it is then your better off.


Adam
PS Having a fixed IP address from your provider makes things easier.






